IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `dog-act-domain`

-- =============================================================================
-- Dog Act 1976 (Western Australia) -- Part VIII, Civil remedies etc.
--
-- Isomorphic encoding of Part VIII ("Civil remedies, etc."), ss 46-47, as at
-- the compilation dated 28 Nov 2024 [version 08-b0-00], re-verified current
-- 2026-09-09. Source: lines 1643-1667 of mrdoc_47983.txt
-- (western-australia/dog-act-1976 source bundle). This is a sibling encoding
-- to dog-act-1976.l4 (Part VI Divisions 1-2) and the other Part-scoped files
-- in this directory; it imports the shared s. 3 domain module
-- (dog-act-domain.l4) for LiableParty (s 46's own "owner"/"deemed owner"
-- facts are read straight off LiableParty's raw fields -- s 46(5)'s deeming
-- rule has no vet/police carve-out, so this file does NOT reuse the s 3
-- derived predicate `the party is a person liable for the control of the
-- dog`, which does) and AttackFacts (s 46(2)'s "in the course of an attack"
-- is exactly the s 3 "attack" definition). Part VIII creates no offence and
-- states no Penalty, so dog-act-domain's Penalty record is not read here.
--
-- SCOPED OUT (cited at its site, never expanded): the Fatal Accidents Act
-- 1959 (WA), referenced by s 46(7) -- a different Act entirely, so its own
-- machinery for maintaining a fatal-accident action is not encoded; this
-- file encodes only the s 46(7) deeming condition itself.
--
-- No regulative (PARTY/MUST/WITHIN) rules appear in this file: neither
-- section states a numeric deadline anywhere -- s 46 creates strict civil
-- liability and defences to it, and s 47 creates a civil debt/recovery
-- right, neither with a "within N days" duty. Every operative provision
-- below is a BOOLEAN predicate (liability, defence, or entitlement), per the
-- assignment's own guidance for deadline-free duties.
--
-- Two genuine ambiguities were found; both are recorded inline at their site
-- and in the returned ambiguities array. One (F-S47-REPRESENTATIVE-
-- QUALIFIER-SCOPE) is materialised below as the `Interpretation` record's
-- field, following this corpus's existing precedent (dog-act-1976.l4's
-- F-DANGEROUS-DOG-STATUS-TIMING) for a fork with observable divergence.
-- =============================================================================

§ `Part VIII -- Civil remedies, etc.`
§§ `46. Damages`

-- s 46(1) -- saving clause: the conviction of a person under this Act does
-- not affect any right or remedy by civil process of any party arising in
-- relation to the same matter, and this Act does not limit or affect any
-- right, remedy or proceeding under any other Act or at law. Not a computed
-- rule -- there is no fact this encoding derives from a saving clause -- so
-- it is recorded here as inert prose, following dog-act-1976.l4's treatment
-- of s 33D(3).

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46(5)
GIVEN party IS A LiableParty
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the party is deemed to be the owner of the dog under s 46(5)` party MEANS
        party's `has the dog in possession or under control`
    OR  party's `is the occupier of premises where the dog is ordinarily kept or ordinarily permitted to live`

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46(2), (5)
GIVEN party IS A LiableParty
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the party is liable in damages as owner or deemed owner under s 46` party MEANS
        party's `is the owner of the dog`
    OR  `the party is deemed to be the owner of the dog under s 46(5)` party

DECLARE DamagesHarm HAS
    `injury to a person or animal was inflicted`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `damage to the property of a person was caused` IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 46(3): a party seeking damages under s 46(2) need not show a previous
-- mischievous propensity in the dog, the owner's (or deemed owner's)
-- knowledge of that propensity, or that the injury was attributable to the
-- owner's (or deemed owner's) neglect. This encoding reflects that rule by
-- OMITTING any such fact from DamagesHarm or from the predicate below -- not
-- by a separate computed rule; there is no propensity/knowledge/neglect
-- field anywhere in this file for the predicate to fail to require.
--
-- "subject to any contributory negligence" (s 46(2)) qualifies the QUANTUM
-- of damages recoverable, not eligibility for the claim itself -- this Act
-- states no contributory-negligence apportionment formula, so it is not
-- computed here; a downstream quantum assessment is a fact/computation this
-- encoding does not attempt.
@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46(2)
GIVEN party IS A LiableParty, attack IS AN AttackFacts, harm IS A DamagesHarm
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the owner is liable in damages under s 46(2)` party attack harm MEANS
        `the party is liable in damages as owner or deemed owner under s 46` party
    AND `the behaviour is an attack` attack
    AND (   harm's `injury to a person or animal was inflicted`
         OR harm's `damage to the property of a person was caused`)

DECLARE PossessionDefenceFacts HAS
    `at the material time, the dog was not in the defendant's possession or control` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the dog was, at that time, in the actual possession or control of some other person, without the defendant's consent (express or implied)` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46(4)
GIVEN f IS A PossessionDefenceFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the "not in possession or control" defence under s 46(4) is established` f MEANS
        f's `at the material time, the dog was not in the defendant's possession or control`
    AND f's `the dog was, at that time, in the actual possession or control of some other person, without the defendant's consent (express or implied)`

-- AMBIGUITY (F-OVER-18-CONSTRUCTION): "some other person over the age of 18
-- years" (s 46(6)) is literally "more than 18", which would exclude a
-- person on their 18th birthday, but this Act elsewhere uses "reached 18
-- years of age" (e.g. s 33GE) to mean "an adult", i.e. 18 or older. Taken
-- reading: delegated to fact-supply -- the field below is a plain atomic
-- BOOLEAN ("the court is satisfied the other identified person was over the
-- age of 18 years"), not a NUMBER compared with a threshold this encoding
-- would have to pick, so the choice between ">18" and ">=18" is left to
-- whoever supplies the fact, applying the correct construction. Rejected:
-- silently computing an age threshold ourselves, which would bake in one
-- reading without flagging it.
@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46(6)
GIVEN party IS A LiableParty, `court satisfied the dog was in fact owned by some other identified person over the age of 18 years` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the s 46(6) occupier's defence is established` party `court satisfied the dog was in fact owned by some other identified person over the age of 18 years` MEANS
        party's `is the occupier of premises where the dog is ordinarily kept or ordinarily permitted to live`
    AND `court satisfied the dog was in fact owned by some other identified person over the age of 18 years`

-- s 46(7): reuses `the owner is liable in damages under s 46(2)` above
-- rather than a fresh independent fact -- "that person would ... have been
-- entitled to maintain an action against, and recover damages from, the
-- owner of the dog in respect of injury caused by the attack" IS exactly a
-- s 46(2) injury-to-a-person claim, so the harm is fixed here to "injury to
-- a person" TRUE / "damage to property" FALSE rather than taken as a fresh
-- input.
@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46(7)
GIVEN `death of a person was caused by the attack of a dog` IS A BOOLEAN, party IS A LiableParty, attack IS AN AttackFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the death is deemed a wrongful act, neglect or default of the owner for the purposes of the Fatal Accidents Act 1959` `death of a person was caused by the attack of a dog` party attack MEANS
        `death of a person was caused by the attack of a dog`
    AND `the owner is liable in damages under s 46(2)` party attack
            (DamagesHarm WITH `injury to a person or animal was inflicted` IS TRUE, `damage to the property of a person was caused` IS FALSE)
        -- Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (WA) -- scoped out; this file encodes only s 46(7)'s deeming condition, not that Act's own machinery

§§ `47. Veterinary service expenses recoverable from local government`

-- AMBIGUITY (F-S47-REPRESENTATIVE-QUALIFIER-SCOPE): s 47(1) defines
-- "representative" as "(a) a person who is an authorised person by virtue
-- of an appointment or designation ...; or (b) an employee of the local
-- government, who, at the relevant time, is performing a function on
-- behalf of the local government." Limb (a) already carries its own
-- self-contained "who is ..." clause; limb (b) does not repeat "who", and
-- the trailing "who, at the relevant time, is performing a function ..."
-- clause follows the whole two-item list rather than sitting inside (b).
-- Two readings compete: the trailing clause could be a common qualifier on
-- BOTH limbs (matching this Act's own convention elsewhere of a trailing
-- clause closing out a limb list, e.g. s 45(1)'s "... is evidence of that
-- fact" closing an (i)-(xiv) list), or it could attach grammatically to
-- limb (b) alone (the closest antecedent, since (a) needs no further
-- qualification -- it is already a complete "a person who is ..." clause).
-- MATERIALISED below as the `S47 Qualifier Scope`/`Interpretation` fields --
-- the readings diverge on a person appointed under (a) who is not currently
-- performing any function for the local government, witnessed by
-- `` `an authorised officer, off duty` `` in the Illustrations. Taken:
-- the qualifier applies to both limbs (the more natural reading of
-- "representative", and the one consistent with this Act's own drafting
-- convention noted above).
DECLARE `S47 Qualifier Scope` IS ONE OF
    qualifierAppliesToBothLimbs
    qualifierAppliesToLimbBOnly

DECLARE Interpretation HAS
    `s 47(1) qualifier scope` IS A `S47 Qualifier Scope`

DECLARE RepresentativeFacts HAS
    `is an authorised person by virtue of an appointment or designation made by the local government` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is an employee of the local government`                                                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `at the relevant time, is performing a function on behalf of the local government`                IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 47(1) "representative"; F-S47-REPRESENTATIVE-QUALIFIER-SCOPE
GIVEN r IS A RepresentativeFacts, interp IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` r interp MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `s 47(1) qualifier scope`
    WHEN qualifierAppliesToBothLimbs THEN
            (   r's `is an authorised person by virtue of an appointment or designation made by the local government`
             OR r's `is an employee of the local government`)
        AND r's `at the relevant time, is performing a function on behalf of the local government`
    WHEN qualifierAppliesToLimbBOnly THEN
            r's `is an authorised person by virtue of an appointment or designation made by the local government`
        OR  (   r's `is an employee of the local government`
             AND r's `at the relevant time, is performing a function on behalf of the local government`)

DECLARE VetServiceRequest HAS
    `veterinary services were requested in respect of a dog` IS A BOOLEAN
    requester                                                IS A RepresentativeFacts

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 47(2)
GIVEN req IS A VetServiceRequest, interp IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the local government is liable to pay for the veterinary services under s 47(2)` req interp MEANS
        req's `veterinary services were requested in respect of a dog`
    AND `the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` (req's requester) interp

DECLARE VetCostClaim HAS
    `veterinarian provided services in respect of a dog` IS A BOOLEAN
    requester                                            IS A RepresentativeFacts
    `amount of the costs for the services`               IS A NUMBER

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 47(3)
GIVEN c IS A VetCostClaim, interp IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the veterinarian may recover the costs from the local government under s 47(3)` c interp MEANS
        c's `veterinarian provided services in respect of a dog`
    AND `the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` (c's requester) interp

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 47(3)
GIVEN c IS A VetCostClaim, interp IS AN Interpretation
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the amount recoverable by the veterinarian under s 47(3)` c interp MEANS
    IF `the veterinarian may recover the costs from the local government under s 47(3)` c interp
    THEN JUST (c's `amount of the costs for the services`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- =============================================================================
-- Illustrations
-- =============================================================================

§ `Illustrations`

`an ordinary dog owner` MEANS LiableParty WITH
    `is the registered owner of the dog` IS TRUE
    , `is the owner of the dog` IS TRUE
    , `is the occupier of premises where the dog is ordinarily kept or ordinarily permitted to live` IS TRUE
    , `has the dog in possession or under control` IS TRUE
    , `is a veterinarian, or acting on a veterinarian's behalf, in professional practice` IS FALSE
    , `is a police officer or other person acting under statutory duty or administering this Act` IS FALSE

`a mere occupier, not the owner` MEANS LiableParty WITH
    `is the registered owner of the dog` IS FALSE
    , `is the owner of the dog` IS FALSE
    , `is the occupier of premises where the dog is ordinarily kept or ordinarily permitted to live` IS TRUE
    , `has the dog in possession or under control` IS FALSE
    , `is a veterinarian, or acting on a veterinarian's behalf, in professional practice` IS FALSE
    , `is a police officer or other person acting under statutory duty or administering this Act` IS FALSE

`a stranger with no connection to the dog` MEANS LiableParty WITH
    `is the registered owner of the dog` IS FALSE
    , `is the owner of the dog` IS FALSE
    , `is the occupier of premises where the dog is ordinarily kept or ordinarily permitted to live` IS FALSE
    , `has the dog in possession or under control` IS FALSE
    , `is a veterinarian, or acting on a veterinarian's behalf, in professional practice` IS FALSE
    , `is a police officer or other person acting under statutory duty or administering this Act` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `the party is deemed to be the owner of the dog under s 46(5)` `a mere occupier, not the owner`
#ASSERT NOT (`the party is deemed to be the owner of the dog under s 46(5)` `a stranger with no connection to the dog`)

#ASSERT `the party is liable in damages as owner or deemed owner under s 46` `an ordinary dog owner`
#ASSERT `the party is liable in damages as owner or deemed owner under s 46` `a mere occupier, not the owner`
#ASSERT NOT (`the party is liable in damages as owner or deemed owner under s 46` `a stranger with no connection to the dog`)

`an unprovoked attack causing injury` MEANS AttackFacts WITH
    `behaviour was an immediate response to, and induced by, provocation` IS FALSE
    , `behaviour aggressively rushed at or harassed a person or animal` IS FALSE
    , `behaviour bit or otherwise caused physical injury` IS TRUE
    , `behaviour tore clothing or caused damage to property` IS FALSE
    , `behaviour was an attempt to attack, or would cause a reasonable person to fear physical injury` IS FALSE
    , `owner establishes the behaviour was justified by a reasonable cause` IS FALSE

`a provoked response, not an attack` MEANS AttackFacts WITH
    `behaviour was an immediate response to, and induced by, provocation` IS TRUE
    , `behaviour aggressively rushed at or harassed a person or animal` IS TRUE
    , `behaviour bit or otherwise caused physical injury` IS TRUE
    , `behaviour tore clothing or caused damage to property` IS FALSE
    , `behaviour was an attempt to attack, or would cause a reasonable person to fear physical injury` IS FALSE
    , `owner establishes the behaviour was justified by a reasonable cause` IS FALSE

`injury only` MEANS DamagesHarm WITH `injury to a person or animal was inflicted` IS TRUE, `damage to the property of a person was caused` IS FALSE
`neither injury nor property damage` MEANS DamagesHarm WITH `injury to a person or animal was inflicted` IS FALSE, `damage to the property of a person was caused` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `the owner is liable in damages under s 46(2)` `an ordinary dog owner` `an unprovoked attack causing injury` `injury only`
#ASSERT NOT (`the owner is liable in damages under s 46(2)` `an ordinary dog owner` `a provoked response, not an attack` `injury only`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the owner is liable in damages under s 46(2)` `a stranger with no connection to the dog` `an unprovoked attack causing injury` `injury only`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the owner is liable in damages under s 46(2)` `an ordinary dog owner` `an unprovoked attack causing injury` `neither injury nor property damage`)

`possession defence, both limbs met` MEANS PossessionDefenceFacts WITH
    `at the material time, the dog was not in the defendant's possession or control` IS TRUE
    , `the dog was, at that time, in the actual possession or control of some other person, without the defendant's consent (express or implied)` IS TRUE

`possession defence, consent was given` MEANS PossessionDefenceFacts WITH
    `at the material time, the dog was not in the defendant's possession or control` IS TRUE
    , `the dog was, at that time, in the actual possession or control of some other person, without the defendant's consent (express or implied)` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `the "not in possession or control" defence under s 46(4) is established` `possession defence, both limbs met`
#ASSERT NOT (`the "not in possession or control" defence under s 46(4) is established` `possession defence, consent was given`)

#ASSERT `the s 46(6) occupier's defence is established` `a mere occupier, not the owner` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`the s 46(6) occupier's defence is established` `a mere occupier, not the owner` FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`the s 46(6) occupier's defence is established` `a stranger with no connection to the dog` TRUE)

#ASSERT `the death is deemed a wrongful act, neglect or default of the owner for the purposes of the Fatal Accidents Act 1959` TRUE `an ordinary dog owner` `an unprovoked attack causing injury`
#ASSERT NOT (`the death is deemed a wrongful act, neglect or default of the owner for the purposes of the Fatal Accidents Act 1959` FALSE `an ordinary dog owner` `an unprovoked attack causing injury`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the death is deemed a wrongful act, neglect or default of the owner for the purposes of the Fatal Accidents Act 1959` TRUE `an ordinary dog owner` `a provoked response, not an attack`)

`taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs` MEANS Interpretation WITH `s 47(1) qualifier scope` IS qualifierAppliesToBothLimbs
`rejected -- qualifier applies to limb (b) only` MEANS Interpretation WITH `s 47(1) qualifier scope` IS qualifierAppliesToLimbBOnly

`an authorised officer, currently on duty` MEANS RepresentativeFacts WITH
    `is an authorised person by virtue of an appointment or designation made by the local government` IS TRUE
    , `is an employee of the local government` IS FALSE
    , `at the relevant time, is performing a function on behalf of the local government` IS TRUE

`an authorised officer, off duty` MEANS RepresentativeFacts WITH
    `is an authorised person by virtue of an appointment or designation made by the local government` IS TRUE
    , `is an employee of the local government` IS FALSE
    , `at the relevant time, is performing a function on behalf of the local government` IS FALSE

`an employee, off duty` MEANS RepresentativeFacts WITH
    `is an authorised person by virtue of an appointment or designation made by the local government` IS FALSE
    , `is an employee of the local government` IS TRUE
    , `at the relevant time, is performing a function on behalf of the local government` IS FALSE

`a stranger to the local government` MEANS RepresentativeFacts WITH
    `is an authorised person by virtue of an appointment or designation made by the local government` IS FALSE
    , `is an employee of the local government` IS FALSE
    , `at the relevant time, is performing a function on behalf of the local government` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` `an authorised officer, currently on duty` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs`
#ASSERT NOT (`the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` `an employee, off duty` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` `a stranger to the local government` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs`)

-- F-S47-REPRESENTATIVE-QUALIFIER-SCOPE witness: an authorised officer who is
-- off duty is a "representative" under the rejected reading (limb (a) needs
-- no performing-function test) but not under the taken reading (the
-- qualifier reaches both limbs).
#ASSERT `the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` `an authorised officer, off duty` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs` EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` `an authorised officer, off duty` `rejected -- qualifier applies to limb (b) only` EQUALS TRUE

-- The two readings agree once the officer is on duty.
#ASSERT `the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` `an authorised officer, currently on duty` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs` EQUALS `the person is a representative of the local government (s 47(1))` `an authorised officer, currently on duty` `rejected -- qualifier applies to limb (b) only`

`a duly requested vet visit` MEANS VetServiceRequest WITH
    `veterinary services were requested in respect of a dog` IS TRUE
    , requester IS `an authorised officer, currently on duty`

`a request from an off-duty employee` MEANS VetServiceRequest WITH
    `veterinary services were requested in respect of a dog` IS TRUE
    , requester IS `an employee, off duty`

#ASSERT `the local government is liable to pay for the veterinary services under s 47(2)` `a duly requested vet visit` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs`
#ASSERT NOT (`the local government is liable to pay for the veterinary services under s 47(2)` `a request from an off-duty employee` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs`)

`a compliant vet cost claim` MEANS VetCostClaim WITH
    `veterinarian provided services in respect of a dog` IS TRUE
    , requester IS `an authorised officer, currently on duty`
    , `amount of the costs for the services` IS 480

`a claim from a stranger's request` MEANS VetCostClaim WITH
    `veterinarian provided services in respect of a dog` IS TRUE
    , requester IS `a stranger to the local government`
    , `amount of the costs for the services` IS 480

#ASSERT `the veterinarian may recover the costs from the local government under s 47(3)` `a compliant vet cost claim` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs`
#ASSERT NOT (`the veterinarian may recover the costs from the local government under s 47(3)` `a claim from a stranger's request` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs`)

#ASSERT `the amount recoverable by the veterinarian under s 47(3)` `a compliant vet cost claim` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs` EQUALS JUST 480
#ASSERT `the amount recoverable by the veterinarian under s 47(3)` `a claim from a stranger's request` `taken -- qualifier applies to both limbs` EQUALS NOTHING
